// AUTO-GENERATED from marketing/content-drafts/song-notes.md — do not hand-edit.
export const NOTES = {"fur-elise":"<p>Für Elise is Beethoven's <em>Bagatelle No. 25 in A minor</em> (WoO 59), dated <strong>27 April 1810</strong> on its now‑lost manuscript and not published until <strong>1867</strong> — about 40 years after his death — when the scholar Ludwig Nohl discovered it. It is a five‑part rondo (A–B–A–C–A): the instantly recognisable theme returns three times between two contrasting episodes. Who \"Elise\" was is still unknown, with scholars proposing Therese Malfatti, Elisabeth Röckel or Elise Barensfeld. The gentle, stepwise opening is one of the most beginner‑friendly passages in the whole repertoire. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F%C3%BCr_Elise\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","clair-de-lune":"<p>\"Clair de lune\" — French for \"moonlight\" — is the third movement of Debussy's <em>Suite bergamasque</em>. He began the suite around <strong>1890</strong> but revised it substantially before its <strong>1905</strong> publication. The title comes from a poem by Paul Verlaine; the movement is marked <em>andante très expressif</em> and written in D♭ major. Its dreamy, unhurried character makes slow practice feel natural — and because letter notes ignore the five‑flat key signature entirely, it's far less daunting to begin than its reputation suggests. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Suite_bergamasque\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","canon-in-d":"<p>Pachelbel's Canon — properly the <em>Canon and Gigue in D major</em> — is a Baroque work by Johann Pachelbel (1653–1706) whose exact date is unknown (suggestions range from 1680 to 1706). It is a true three‑part canon over a repeating ground bass, and that endlessly looping chord progression is why it has shaped countless later pop songs. Remarkably, the piece stayed in obscurity for centuries and only became famous after a 1968 recording; today it is a wedding‑ceremony staple worldwide. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pachelbel%27s_Canon\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","moonlight-sonata":"<p>The \"Moonlight Sonata\" is Beethoven's <em>Piano Sonata No. 14 in C♯ minor, Op. 27 No. 2</em> (subtitled <em>Quasi una fantasia</em>), completed in <strong>1801</strong> and published in <strong>1802</strong> with a dedication to his pupil Countess Giulietta Guicciardi. Beethoven never called it \"Moonlight\" — the nickname spread after his death, from an 1824 comparison by the poet Ludwig Rellstab. The famous first movement (<em>Adagio sostenuto</em>) is played very quietly throughout; the later movements grow much more demanding. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Piano_Sonata_No._14_(Beethoven\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","rondo-alla-turca":"<p>The \"Rondo Alla Turca\" (Turkish March) is the finale of Mozart's <em>Piano Sonata No. 11 in A major, K. 331</em>, composed around <strong>1783</strong> and published in <strong>1784</strong>. Mozart himself headed the movement \"Alla turca\": it imitates the sound of Turkish Janissary bands, whose music was hugely fashionable in Vienna at the time. In 2014 several long‑lost pages of Mozart's own manuscript were rediscovered in a Budapest library. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Piano_Sonata_No._11_(Mozart\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","gymnopedie-no-1":"<p>Erik Satie completed his three <em>Gymnopédies</em> in <strong>1888</strong>, publishing the first that year. No. 1 is marked <em>Lent et douloureux</em> (\"slow and painful\"), in 3/4 time, and the title nods to the <em>gymnopaedia</em>, an ancient Greek festival dance. Its gentle, hovering dissonances create a famously calm, melancholy mood — and because the tempo is meant to be slow, beginners aren't fighting the clock. Debussy admired the set enough to orchestrate two of them in 1897. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gymnop%C3%A9dies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","the-entertainer":"<p>Scott Joplin's \"The Entertainer\" is a classic piano rag, subtitled \"A Rag Time Two Step,\" copyrighted in <strong>December 1902</strong>. Largely forgotten for decades, it roared back in 1973 when Marvin Hamlisch's adaptation for the film <em>The Sting</em> reached No. 3 on the Billboard Hot 100 and won an Academy Award. The RIAA later placed it tenth on its \"Songs of the Century\" list. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Entertainer_(rag\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","nocturne-op-9-no-2":"<p>Chopin wrote his three <em>Nocturnes, Op. 9</em> in <strong>1831–32</strong> (published 1832), dedicating them to Madame Marie Pleyel. No. 2 in E♭ major — often considered his single most famous piece — unfolds like a gentle waltz in 12/8, its songful melody growing more ornately decorated on each return. Chopin was only about twenty when he composed it. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nocturnes,_Op._9_(Chopin\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","ave-maria":"<p>Schubert composed this melody in <strong>1825</strong> — but not, originally, as a prayer. It was a setting of \"Ellen's third song\" from a German translation of Walter Scott's poem <em>The Lady of the Lake</em>, in which the character Ellen sings to the Virgin Mary. Because her song opens with the words \"Ave Maria,\" people later fitted the traditional Latin prayer to Schubert's tune, and that pairing became so common it's now assumed he wrote it that way. Disney's <em>Fantasia</em> (1940) helped cement its fame. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ellens_dritter_Gesang\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","air-on-the-g-string":"<p>The music began as the \"Air,\" the second movement of Bach's <em>Orchestral Suite No. 3 in D major</em> (BWV 1068), from the early 1730s. The beloved \"Air on the G String\" version is an <strong>1871</strong> arrangement by the violinist August Wilhelmj, who transposed it so the whole melody can be played on the violin's lowest string — the G string — which is exactly where the nickname comes from. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Air_on_the_G_String\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","blue-danube":"<p>\"The Blue Danube\" (<em>An der schönen blauen Donau</em>, Op. 314) is Johann Strauss II's most famous waltz, composed in <strong>1866</strong> and premiered in February <strong>1867</strong> in Vienna. It was written first with choral lyrics; the purely orchestral version made for the 1867 Paris World's Fair is what conquered the world. Brahms once signed a lady's fan with its opening bars, adding \"Unfortunately not by Johannes Brahms.\" <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Blue_Danube\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","hall-of-the-mountain-king":"<p>Grieg composed \"In the Hall of the Mountain King\" in <strong>1875</strong> as part of his incidental music for Ibsen's play <em>Peer Gynt</em>; it later became the finale of the <em>Peer Gynt Suite No. 1</em>. In the play it accompanies Peer fleeing through the troll king's hall, pursued by a horde of trolls — which is why the music starts quietly and accelerates into an ever louder, faster, more frenzied chase. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/In_the_Hall_of_the_Mountain_King\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","maple-leaf-rag":"<p>Scott Joplin's \"Maple Leaf Rag,\" copyrighted in <strong>1899</strong>, is one of the most famous ragtime pieces ever written and became the model that later rag composers followed. Its success earned Joplin his title, the \"King of Ragtime,\" and provided him a steady income for the rest of his life. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maple_Leaf_Rag\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","toccata-and-fugue":"<p>The <em>Toccata and Fugue in D minor, BWV 565</em> is one of the most recognisable works in the organ repertoire, famous for its dramatic opening flourish. It has long been attributed to J. S. Bach, though a minority of scholars have questioned that since 1981; the leading Bach scholar Christoph Wolff maintains it is genuinely Bach's. Disney's <em>Fantasia</em> (1940) launched its fame, and it has since become film shorthand for horror and villainy. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Toccata_and_Fugue_in_D_minor,_BWV_565\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","fantaisie-impromptu":"<p>Chopin composed the <em>Fantaisie‑Impromptu</em> in C♯ minor (Op. 66) in <strong>1834</strong>, but never published it — he had left instructions that his unpublished manuscripts not be released. It appeared in <strong>1855</strong>, after his death, against those wishes, and became one of his most popular works. Its dreamy middle melody later resurfaced in the popular song \"I'm Always Chasing Rainbows.\" <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fantaisie-Impromptu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","carmen-habanera":"<p>The \"Habanera\" is the entrance aria of the title character in Bizet's <strong>1875</strong> opera <em>Carmen</em> — a habanera being a dance from Havana. Bizet built it on a melody by Sebastián Iradier that he mistook for a folk song; on learning it had a composer, he acknowledged the source in his printed score. It is one of the most recognisable tunes in all of opera. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Habanera_(aria\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","eine-kleine-nachtmusik":"<p><em>Eine kleine Nachtmusik</em> — \"a little night music\" — is Mozart's serenade for strings, K. 525, completed in Vienna in <strong>1787</strong>. One of the most iconic works in all of classical music, it wasn't published until 1827, more than 30 years after his death, and a fifth movement is believed to have been lost. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eine_kleine_Nachtmusik\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","swan-lake":"<p>Tchaikovsky composed the ballet <em>Swan Lake</em> in <strong>1875–76</strong>; it premiered at Moscow's Bolshoi Theatre in <strong>1877</strong>. Critics initially found it \"too noisy\" and \"too symphonic,\" and it was judged a failure — yet it is now one of the most beloved ballets ever written. The version companies dance today descends largely from an 1895 St. Petersburg revival. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swan_Lake\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","wedding-march":"<p>Mendelssohn wrote his famous \"Wedding March\" in C major as part of his <strong>1842</strong> incidental music to Shakespeare's <em>A Midsummer Night's Dream</em>. It became the standard wedding recessional after Victoria, the Princess Royal, chose it for her 1858 marriage — although the earliest documented wedding use dates back to 1847. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wedding_March_(Mendelssohn\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","sugar-plum-fairy":"<p>The \"Dance of the Sugar Plum Fairy\" comes from Tchaikovsky's <strong>1892</strong> ballet <em>The Nutcracker</em>. Its shimmering, bell‑like sound comes from the celesta — a then‑novel keyboard instrument Tchaikovsky had discovered — which gives the dance its unmistakable, glassy magic. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dance_of_the_Sugar_Plum_Fairy\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","humoresque-7":"<p>Dvořák's <em>Humoresque No. 7</em> in G♭ major comes from his set of eight <em>Humoresques</em>, Op. 101, written in <strong>1894</strong>. One critic called it \"probably the most famous small piano work ever written after Beethoven's Für Elise.\" In America it became so familiar that people fitted comic verses to its tune. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Humoresques_(Dvo%C5%99%C3%A1k\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","liebestraum-3":"<p>Liszt's <em>Liebestraum No. 3</em> in A♭ major is the most famous of his three <em>Liebesträume</em> (\"Dreams of Love\"), published in <strong>1850</strong>. They began life as songs set to Romantic poems; Liszt issued them simultaneously as vocal pieces and as these solo‑piano transcriptions. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liebestr%C3%A4ume\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","raindrop-prelude":"<p>The \"Raindrop\" is No. 15 of Chopin's 24 <em>Preludes</em>, Op. 28, in D♭ major — the longest of the set. He wrote it in <strong>1838</strong> while staying at a monastery in Valldemossa, Mallorca. The nickname comes from a repeated note many hear as falling rain, though Chopin himself rejected the idea of literal imitation. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prelude,_Op._28,_No._15_(Chopin\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","arabesque-1":"<p>Debussy composed his two <em>Arabesques</em> while still in his twenties, between <strong>1888 and 1891</strong>. No. 1, in E major, is among the earliest pieces to hint at the Impressionist style he would become famous for — flowing, gentle and free. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deux_arabesques\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","flight-of-the-bumblebee":"<p>\"Flight of the Bumblebee\" is an orchestral interlude from Rimsky‑Korsakov's opera <em>The Tale of Tsar Saltan</em> (<strong>1899–1900</strong>), written to evoke a bumblebee's frantic buzzing. Its near‑continuous rush of rapid chromatic notes has made it a favourite showpiece for demonstrating a performer's speed. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flight_of_the_Bumblebee\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","goldberg-aria":"<p>Bach's <em>Goldberg Variations</em> — an aria followed by thirty variations — was published in <strong>1741</strong>, unusually during his own lifetime. A famous legend (recorded some 60 years later, and widely doubted) holds that it was written to soothe a sleepless count, played to him by the young harpsichordist Johann Gottlieb Goldberg. Every third variation is a canon. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goldberg_Variations\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","prelude-in-c":"<p>This is the very first piece in Bach's <em>The Well‑Tempered Clavier</em>, Book I — the <em>Prelude in C major, BWV 846</em> (Bach's autograph is dated <strong>1722</strong>). It is built almost entirely from gently rolling broken chords in a steady pattern, which makes it hypnotic to play and surprisingly approachable. Charles Gounod later wrote a melody over these exact harmonies, creating the famous \"Ave Maria.\" <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prelude_and_Fugue_in_C_major,_BWV_846\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","morning-mood":"<p>\"Morning Mood\" (<em>Morgenstemning</em>) is the serene E‑major prelude to Act IV of Grieg's incidental music for Ibsen's <em>Peer Gynt</em>, composed in <strong>1875</strong>, and it opens his <em>Peer Gynt Suite No. 1</em>. Its gently rising melody is one of the most instantly recognisable evocations of a sunrise in all of music. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peer_Gynt_(Grieg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","revolutionary-etude":"<p>Chopin's <em>Étude in C minor, Op. 10 No. 12</em>, written around <strong>1831</strong>, is nicknamed the \"Revolutionary Étude.\" A popular legend links it to the failed November 1830 Polish uprising against Russia — though that story is apocryphal. Whatever its origin, its storming left‑hand torrent beneath a defiant melody makes it one of his most dramatic studies. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Revolutionary_Etude\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","pavane-faure":"<p>Fauré composed his <em>Pavane</em>, Op. 50, in <strong>1887</strong> — a pavane being a slow, stately processional dance. He wrote it first for piano and chorus, then orchestrated it; it was dedicated to his patron, the Comtesse Greffulhe, and has been beloved ever since for its cool, elegant melody. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pavane_(Faur%C3%A9\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","nocturne-op-27-no-2":"<p>Chopin's <em>Nocturne in D♭ major, Op. 27 No. 2</em>, composed in <strong>1836</strong> and published in <strong>1837</strong>, is one of his most admired. Its lyrical, songlike melody is spun out with increasingly intricate ornamentation over a flowing accompaniment, building to a cadence often called one of the most glorious moments in all his music. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nocturnes,_Op._27_(Chopin\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","waltz-of-the-flowers":"<p>The \"Waltz of the Flowers\" closes Act II of Tchaikovsky's <strong>1892</strong> ballet <em>The Nutcracker</em> (Op. 71). A grand, sweeping waltz, it is one of the most famous numbers from a score that has become inseparable from Christmas around the world. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Waltz_of_the_Flowers\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","gnossienne-no-1":"<p>The <em>Gnossiennes</em> are a set of enigmatic piano pieces by Erik Satie, the first composed around <strong>1890</strong>. Satie wrote them without time signatures or bar lines — \"free time\" — and sprinkled the score with strange written directions such as \"with astonishment.\" He even invented the word <em>gnossienne</em> as a title. Their slow, hypnotic strangeness makes them unforgettable. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gnossiennes\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","ode-to-joy":"<p>This is the melody Beethoven set in the finale of his <em>Symphony No. 9</em>, completed in <strong>1824</strong>, to words from Friedrich Schiller's 1785 poem \"An die Freude\" (\"To Joy\"). The tune — though not Schiller's text — was adopted as the official <strong>Anthem of Europe</strong> in 1972. Its stepwise, neighbour‑note melody is exactly what makes it such a perfect first piece. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ode_to_Joy\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","twinkle-twinkle":"<p>The famous tune is actually an old French melody, \"Ah! vous dirai‑je, maman,\" which first appeared in <strong>1761</strong> — it isn't an original composition. Mozart wrote a celebrated set of <em>Twelve Variations</em> (K. 265) on it around <strong>1781–82</strong>, turning the simple nursery tune into a dazzling showpiece. The English \"Twinkle, Twinkle, Little Star\" words came later. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twelve_Variations_on_%22Ah_vous_dirai-je,_Maman%22\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","amazing-grace":"<p>\"Amazing Grace\" is a Christian hymn whose words were written by <strong>John Newton</strong> — a former slave‑trader who became an abolitionist clergyman — in late <strong>1772</strong>, published in <strong>1779</strong>. The tune everyone knows today, called \"New Britain,\" wasn't paired with the words until <strong>1835</strong>. Its slow, singable line makes it ideal early practice. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amazing_Grace\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","minuet-in-g":"<p>This famous little minuet in G major (BWV Anh. 114) comes from the <strong>1725</strong> <em>Notebook for Anna Magdalena Bach</em> and was for centuries attributed to J. S. Bach — but it is now credited to the Dresden composer <strong>Christian Petzold</strong> (c. 1720). One of the most‑taught beginner pieces ever written, its melody also became the 1965 pop hit \"A Lover's Concerto.\" <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minuet_in_G_major_(Petzold\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>"};
